      <h1>Cycle &amp; Safety Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>Cycle-life testing</strong> repeatedly charges and discharges a battery
        under defined conditions and counts the cycles until capacity falls below a threshold (commonly 80% of
        original). <strong>Safety testing</strong> deliberately stresses a battery past its limits — overcharge, short
        circuit, crush, puncture, thermal abuse — to verify it fails safely rather than catastrophically. Cycle life
        is a lifespan number; safety testing is a "does it fail safely" number.
      </div>

      <h2>How cycle life is measured</h2>
      <p>
        Cycle life is always quoted against a specific <strong>depth of discharge (DoD)</strong>, temperature and charge
        rate. A shallow cycle (20% DoD) yields far more cycles than a deep one (80% DoD), which is why a bare
        "2,000 cycles" claim is meaningless without its conditions. The result is reported as the cycle count at
        which capacity falls to a threshold — typically <strong>80% of original</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>What cycle-life conditions matter</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Effect on cycle count</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Depth of discharge</td><td>Deeper discharge → fewer cycles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Heat accelerates aging → fewer cycles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Charge/discharge rate (C-rate)</td><td>Faster → more heat and stress → fewer cycles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Charge voltage</td><td>Over-voltage → faster degradation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Safety (abuse) tests</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Overcharge</strong> — charging past the voltage limit to confirm it does not ignite.</li>
        <li><strong>Short circuit</strong> — a direct short to confirm the protection responds.</li>
        <li><strong>Crush / puncture</strong> — mechanical damage to confirm no thermal runaway propagation.</li>
        <li><strong>Thermal</strong> — external heat to confirm the cell stays within its temperature limits.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are codified in standards such as <strong>UN 38.3</strong> (transport), <strong>IEC 62133</strong> and
        <strong>IEC 62660</strong> (cell safety). A battery that passes abuse testing is not "impossible to fail" — it has
        been shown to fail in a controlled way under defined abuse.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My take:</strong> A cycle-life number without its depth-of-discharge is marketing, and a "safety
        certified" claim without the named standard is vague — both need their conditions to mean anything.</p>
        <p><strong>What drives this:</strong> Cycle count and safety are both condition-dependent, yet they are routinely quoted as
        bare numbers. The buyer who asks "at what DoD, temperature and rate" for cycle life, and "which standard" for
        safety, is the one who actually gets a comparable figure.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My view as an editor, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

